-- url     : https://prove2.me/theorems/0f74eb21-cd2b-4d0c-a8c9-24a46079b091
-- title:
--   Integral equations with reflection at an upper barrier $\kappa$: the deterministic map (111) and the reflected diffusion (9)–(10)
-- statement:
--   This module defines solutions of the integral equations with an upper reflecting barrier $\kappa\in\mathbb R$ used in Pang, Talreja and Whitt (2007), Theorem 7.3 (p. 250) and Theorem 1.2 (p. 198). A path is in $D$ if it is right-continuous with left limits on $[0,\infty)$.
--
--   1. **Pushing only at the barrier.** For paths $x$ and $u$, with $u$ nondecreasing and nonnegative, the condition
--   $$
--   \int_0^\infty \mathbf 1\{x(t)<\kappa\}\,du(t)=0
--   $$
--   means that the Lebesgue–Stieltjes measure $du$ of $u$, extended by $u(t)=0$ for $t<0$, gives zero mass to $\{t\ge 0 : x(t)<\kappa\}$. A jump $u(0)>0$ is an atom of $du$ at $0$, so it is allowed only when $x(0)=\kappa$.
--   2. **Deterministic reflected integral representation** (111). Given $h:\mathbb R\to\mathbb R$, $b\in\mathbb R$ and $y\in D$, a pair $(x,u)$ of paths in $D$ solves (111) if $x(t)\le\kappa$ for $t\ge 0$, $u$ is nondecreasing and nonnegative, $u$ pushes only at the barrier, and for every $t\ge0$
--   $$
--   x(t)=b+y(t)+\int_0^t h(x(s))\,ds-u(t).
--   $$
--   3. **Reflected diffusion** (9)–(10). Let $\mu,\beta,\theta\in\mathbb R$ and let $\nu$ be a probability law on $\mathbb R$. A tuple $(\Omega,P,B,X,U)$ is a solution with initial law $\nu$ if $P$ is a probability measure, $B$ is a standard Brownian motion, $X(0)$ has law $\nu$ and is independent of $B$, $X$ and $U$ are adapted to the filtration generated by $X(0)$ and $B$, every path of $X$ and $U$ is in $D$, $X$ takes values in $(-\infty,\kappa]$, $U$ is nondecreasing and nonnegative, and almost surely $U$ pushes only when $X$ is at $\kappa$ and, for every $t\ge 0$,
--   $$
--   X(t)=X(0)-\beta\mu t+\sqrt{2\mu}\,B(t)-\int_0^t\big[\mu(X(s)\wedge 0)+\theta(X(s)\vee 0)\big]\,ds-U(t).
--   $$
--
--   The deterministic map is the tool that turns the martingale representation of the queue into the limit theorem; the reflected diffusion is the limit of Theorem 1.2.
--
--   **Formalization Note** The drift of (9) is written with `ErlangA.Diffusion.drift μ β θ x`, which equals $-\beta\mu-\mu(x\wedge0)-\theta(x\vee0)$. Brownian motion is `ErlangA.Diffusion.IsStandardBM` (time $\mathbb R_{\ge0}$), and adaptedness is `ErlangA.Diffusion.IsAdaptedToInitialAndBM`. The barrier condition is stated with a `StieltjesFunction` equal to $u$ on $[0,\infty)$ and to $0$ on $(-\infty,0)$. Paths are functions on $\mathbb R$ whose values at negative times are ignored. The independence of $X(0)$ and $B$ is implicit in the paper and stated here. Taking (9) at $t=0$ forces $U(0)=0$ almost surely; in (111) $u(0)=(b+y(0)-\kappa)^+$ may be positive.
-- source:
--   Pang, Talreja & Whitt, Martingale Proofs of Many-Server Heavy-Traffic Limits for Markovian Queues, arXiv:0712.4211v1 (reprint of Probab. Surveys 4 (2007) 193–267), p. 198, Theorem 1.2, (9)–(10); p. 250, Theorem 7.3, (111)

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_BellWilliams2001_ThresholdPolicy_Paths
import Definitions.Def_ErlangA_Diffusion_SDE

open MeasureTheory ProbabilityTheory Filter Topology
open scoped ENNReal NNReal

namespace MartingaleHT.FiniteWaiting

open BellWilliams2001.ThresholdPolicy ErlangA.Diffusion

/-!
Pang, Talreja & Whitt (2007), Theorem 7.3 (p. 250) and Theorem 1.2, (9)–(10) (p. 198): integral
equations with reflection at an upper barrier `κ`, deterministic and stochastic.

Conventions. Time is real and every condition quantifies over `t ≥ 0`; values of a path at
negative times are ignored. The regulator `u` is extended by `0` to `t < 0`, so the
Lebesgue–Stieltjes measure `du` carries an atom of mass `u(0)` at `0`; the condition
`∫₀^∞ 1{x(t) < κ} du(t) = 0` is "`du` gives zero mass to `{t ≥ 0 : x(t) < κ}`".
-/

/-- `u` **increases only when `x` is at the barrier `κ`**: `∫₀^∞ 1{x(t) < κ} du(t) = 0`. There
is a Stieltjes function `F` equal to `u` on `[0, ∞)` and to `0` on `(−∞, 0)` (so a jump
`u(0) > 0` is an atom of `dF` at `0`), and `dF{t ≥ 0 : x(t) < κ} = 0`. -/
def PushesOnlyAtBarrier (κ : ℝ) (x u : ℝ → ℝ) : Prop :=
  ∃ F : StieltjesFunction ℝ, (∀ t : ℝ, 0 ≤ t → F t = u t) ∧ (∀ t : ℝ, t < 0 → F t = 0) ∧
    F.measure {t | 0 ≤ t ∧ x t < κ} = 0

/-- `(x, u)` **solves the integral representation with reflection (111)** of Theorem 7.3 for
data `(y, b)`, drift `h` and barrier `κ`:
* `x` and `u` are in `D` (right-continuous with left limits on `[0, ∞)`);
* `x(t) ≤ κ` for every `t ≥ 0`;
* `u` is nondecreasing and nonnegative on `[0, ∞)`;
* `x(t) = b + y(t) + ∫₀ᵗ h(x(s)) ds − u(t)` for every `t ≥ 0` (111);
* `∫₀^∞ 1{x(t) < κ} du(t) = 0` (`PushesOnlyAtBarrier`). -/
def IsReflectedIntegralSolution (κ : ℝ) (h : ℝ → ℝ) (b : ℝ) (y x u : ℝ → ℝ) : Prop :=
  IsCadlag x ∧ IsCadlag u ∧
  (∀ t : ℝ, 0 ≤ t → x t ≤ κ) ∧
  MonotoneOn u (Set.Ici 0) ∧ (∀ t : ℝ, 0 ≤ t → 0 ≤ u t) ∧
  (∀ t : ℝ, 0 ≤ t → x t = b + y t + (∫ s in (0 : ℝ)..t, h (x s)) - u t) ∧
  PushesOnlyAtBarrier κ x u

/-- `(Ω, P, B, X, U)` is a **solution of the reflected diffusion equation (9)–(10) with initial
law `ν`** (Theorem 1.2, p. 198):
* `P` is a probability measure and `B` is a standard Brownian motion;
* `X(0)` is a random variable with law `ν`, independent of `B`;
* `X` and `U` are adapted to the filtration generated by `X(0)` and `B`;
* every path of `X` and of `U` is in `D`; `X` is `(−∞, κ]`-valued; `U` is nondecreasing and
  nonnegative;
* almost surely, for every `t ≥ 0`,
  `X(t) = X(0) − βµt + √(2µ) B(t) − ∫₀ᵗ [µ(X(s) ∧ 0) + θ(X(s) ∨ 0)] ds − U(t)` (9), written with
  `ErlangA.Diffusion.drift µ β θ x = −βµ − µ(x ∧ 0) − θ(x ∨ 0)`, and
  `∫₀^∞ 1{X(t) < κ} dU(t) = 0` (10). -/
def IsReflectedSolution {Ω : Type*} [MeasurableSpace Ω] (P : Measure Ω) (μ β θ κ : ℝ)
    (ν : Measure ℝ) (B : ℝ≥0 → Ω → ℝ) (X U : Ω → ℝ → ℝ) : Prop :=
  IsProbabilityMeasure P ∧
  IsStandardBM P B ∧
  Measurable (fun ω => X ω 0) ∧
  P.map (fun ω => X ω 0) = ν ∧
  IndepFun (fun ω => X ω 0) (fun ω (t : ℝ≥0) => B t ω) P ∧
  IsAdaptedToInitialAndBM B (fun ω => X ω 0) X ∧
  IsAdaptedToInitialAndBM B (fun ω => X ω 0) U ∧
  (∀ ω, IsCadlag (X ω) ∧ IsCadlag (U ω)) ∧
  (∀ ω (t : ℝ), 0 ≤ t → X ω t ≤ κ) ∧
  (∀ ω, MonotoneOn (U ω) (Set.Ici 0) ∧ ∀ t : ℝ, 0 ≤ t → 0 ≤ U ω t) ∧
  ∀ᵐ ω ∂P,
    (∀ t : ℝ, 0 ≤ t →
      X ω t = X ω 0 + (∫ s in (0 : ℝ)..t, drift μ β θ (X ω s)) +
        Real.sqrt (2 * μ) * B t.toNNReal ω - U ω t) ∧
    PushesOnlyAtBarrier κ (X ω) (U ω)

end MartingaleHT.FiniteWaiting


